{
	"nodes":[
		{
			"id":"37cfbd2e261e63c2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Analysis\nAnalysis is difficult because not all of the indicators are accurate. In a perfect world each indicator should be analysed to find out if its accurate. This is nearly impossible due to the amount and variety of incidents.\n\nNetwork and system profiling can help with this issue. Profiling is measuring the characteristics of expected activity in networking devices and systems so that changes to it can be more easily identified.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-60,
			"y":600,
			"width":549,
			"height":480
		},
		{
			"id":"f01b7e43a4172128",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Post-Incident\n- Review the incident and how it was handled\n- Hold a lessons learned meeting, ask questions such as:\n\t- Exactly what happened and when?\n\t- How well did the staff and management perform while dealing with the incident?\n\t- Were the documented procedures followed? Were they adequate?\n\t- What information was needed sooner?\n\t- Were any steps or actions taken that might have inhibited the recovery?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-60,
			"y":1140,
			"width":549,
			"height":480
		},
		{
			"id":"5970cfbed3495198",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Preparation\n- Organisational processes are created to address communication between people on the response team.\n- Facilities to host the response team and the SOC are created\n- Necessary hardware and software for incident analysis and mitigation is acquired\n- Risk assessments are used to implement controls that will limit the number of incidents\n- Validation of security hardware and software deployment is performed on end-user devices, servers and network devices\n- User security awareness training materials are developed",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1240,
			"y":600,
			"width":540,
			"height":480
		},
		{
			"id":"0fda37074ad4dc44",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Detection\nDetection might be the hardest part of the IR process. Incidents are detected in many different ways and not all of these ways are every detailed or provide detailed clarity.\n\nThere are two categories for the signs of an incident:\n- **Precurosr** - This is a sign that an incident might occur in the future. For example, log entries showing port scan or a newly-discovered vuln.\n- **Indicator** - This is a sign an incident might already have occurred or is current occurring. Examples being a host infected with malware, multiple failed logins from an unknown source or an IDS alert.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-649,
			"y":600,
			"width":549,
			"height":480
		},
		{
			"id":"b056cefbcc172bea",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Containment\nFor every type of incident a containment strategy should be created and enforced.\n\nConditions to determine the type of strategy to create for each incident type:\n- How long it will take to implement and complete a solution?\n- How much time and how many resources will be needed to implement the strategy?\n- What is the process to preserve evidence?\n- Can an attacker be redirected to a sandbox so that the IR team can safely document the attackers methodology?\n- What will be the impact to the availability of services?\n- What is the extend of damage to resources or assets?\n- How effective is the strategy?\n\nThings can get worse during containment...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1240,
			"y":1140,
			"width":549,
			"height":480
		},
		{
			"id":"fd6c6bfd9a08fe06",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Eradication, Recovery & Remediation\nIdentify all the hosts that need remediation. All effects of the incident must be eliminated.\n\n- Recover hosts using clean and recent backups or rebuild them with installation media.\n- Fully patch the OS and software installed.\n- Change all passwords\n- Validate network security, backup strategies and security policies.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-640,
			"y":1140,
			"width":549,
			"height":480
		},
		{
			"id":"22dbf0ea56ee6859",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Incident Notification\nWhen an incident is analysed and prioritised the IR team needs to notify the appropriate stakeholders and outside parties. These typically are:\n- Chief Information Officer (CIO)\n- Head of information security\n- Local information security officer\n- Other IR teams within the org\n- External IR teams (if appropriate)\n- System owner\n- Human resources (for cases involving employees)\n- Public affairs (for incidents that may generate publicity)\n- Legal department (for incidents with potential legal ramifications)\n- Authorities (if appropriate)\n- OAIC (AUS)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":661,
			"y":580,
			"width":589,
			"height":520
		},
		{
			"id":"e7a55f6e3cde00db",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Containment, Eradication and Recovery",
			"styleAttributes":{"textAlign":"center"},
			"x":-173,
			"y":160,
			"width":359,
			"height":150
		},
		{
			"id":"13915ef35a2591ce",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Post-Incident Activity",
			"styleAttributes":{"textAlign":"center"},
			"x":387,
			"y":160,
			"width":359,
			"height":150
		},
		{
